 # Value compression
 
+Also called: Block-based compression, Block Truncation Coding (BTC)
 
 ## Overview
-Value compression encompasses methods that reduce the storage and bandwidth requirements of volumetric scalar fields by transforming, quantizing, or encoding the data values themselves, rather than relying solely on geometric simplification or structural sparsity. It solves the problem of high memory footprint and transfer latency in real-time volume rendering by exploiting spatial redundancy, frequency coherence, or statistical properties of the data. It sets itself apart from [Structural and Geometric Compression](4959-structural-and-geometric-compression.md) by operating on the full volumetric grid (or dense transforms thereof) rather than surface meshes, and from [Standard Image Compression Formats](4394-standard-image-compression-formats.md) by often utilizing 3D-specific transforms or learned dictionaries rather than fixed 2D block formats, although it frequently adapts these formats for GPU compatibility.
+Value compression refers to a family of lossy and lossless techniques that reduce the storage and bandwidth requirements of volumetric data by exploiting local spatial redundancy, typically through block partitioning, prediction, or transform-based decorrelation [S1, S4]. It solves the problem of high memory footprint and transfer latency in real-time volume rendering by allowing hardware-accelerated decompression or fast random access to specific regions of the volume [S1]. What sets this family apart from global transform methods (like [Wavelet and Transform-based Compression](6879-wavelet-and-transform-based-compression.md)) is its reliance on fixed-size, data-independent blocks or local statistical properties, which enables constant-time decoding and direct integration with GPU texture hardware, albeit often at the cost of global scalability [S1, S4].
 
