-The decompression process is the reverse of the 3 stages mentioned above (Data Conversion, Quantization, Entropy Coding) [S6, unofficial].
+### General Lossy Compression Flow
+The decompression process is the reverse of the three stages of lossy compression: data transformation, quantization, and entropy coding [S6, unofficial].
 
-#### 4×4×4 Block Reconstruction
-A block-wise decoding optimization for wavelet-compressed volumes that processes voxels in 4×4×4 blocks to reuse R-slice values across adjacent blocks, reducing decoding overhead for spatially coherent access patterns [S7].
-1.  Partition the volume into 4×4×4 blocks, each structurally containing 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice.
-2.  Begin decoding a block by computing the 4×4 voxels of its R-slice.
-3.  Compute the 4×4 voxels for each of the 3 I-slices in the current block by reusing the retained R-slice values from the block above [S7].
-Block dimensions: 4 × 4 × 4 voxels. Block slice composition: 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7]. Purely random access patterns negate the block reuse optimization; the method assumes regular or spatially coherent access [S7]. Random voxel access slowdown: compressed data is 3 to 5 times slower than uncompressed data on average [S7]. Whole-volume reconstruction overhead: compressed volumes take roughly 30-40 seconds longer than original volumes [S7]. Random access is 3-5x slower for compressed data, but this is acceptable compared to disk/network latency and typical non-random access patterns [S7].
+### 4×4×4 Block Reconstruction
+4×4×4 Block Reconstruction is a block-wise decoding optimization for wavelet-compressed volumes that processes voxels in 4×4×4 blocks to reuse R-slice values across adjacent blocks, reducing decoding overhead for spatially coherent access patterns [S7]. The volume is partitioned into 4×4×4 blocks, each containing 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7]. Decoding begins by computing the 4×4 voxels of the R-slice, then computes the 4×4 voxels for each of the 3 I-slices in the current block by reusing the retained R-slice values from the block above [S7]. This optimization assumes regular or spatially coherent access; purely random access patterns negate the benefit [S7]. Random voxel access on compressed data is 3 to 5 times slower than uncompressed data on average, and whole-volume reconstruction takes roughly 30-40 seconds longer than original volumes [S7]. However, this slowdown is acceptable compared to disk/network latency [S7].
 
-#### µ-law Feature Encoding
-A non-linear transformation applied to raw sensor values before computing pixel-wise MSE loss, compressing dynamic range to emphasize shadow detail [S8]. Raw 14-bit sensor values $x$ are transformed as $f(x) = \log(1 + \mu \cdot x/w) / \log(1 + \mu)$, with $\mu = 5000$ and $w = 2^{14} - 1 = 16,383$ (the white level of 14-bit data) [S8].
+### µ-law Feature Encoding
+µ-law Feature Encoding is a non-linear transformation applied to raw sensor values before computing pixel-wise MSE loss, compressing dynamic range to emphasize shadow detail [S8]. Raw 14-bit sensor values $x$ are transformed as $f(x) = \log(1 + \mu \cdot x/w) / \log(1 + \mu)$, with $\mu = 5000$ and $w = 2^{14} - 1 = 16,383$ (the white level of 14-bit data) [S8].
+
+### Entropy Coding
+Entropy coding involves arranging quantized DCT coefficients in a '3D zigzag' order, employing Run-Length Encoding (RLE) to group similar frequencies (zeros) together, inserting length coding zeros, and then using Huffman coding for the remainder [S9]. DC coefficients are coded separately from AC coefficients [S9]. DC coefficients are coded using Differential Pulse Code Modulation (DPCM): the difference between the current DC coefficient and the previous DC coefficient is encoded (except for the first one) [S9]. AC coefficients are coded using RLC (Run-Length Coding) method [S9]. Huffman coding is used to further compress the DPCM-coded DC coefficients and the RLE-coded AC coefficients [S9].
 
 ## Background
-Value compression for volume rendering has evolved from simple scalar quantization to complex transform-based and hardware-accelerated methods. Early work focused on lossless compression using entropy coding and run-length encoding [S1]. The introduction of wavelet transforms allowed for multi-resolution representations, enabling progressive transmission and adaptive rendering [S1]. Hardware support for texture compression (e.g., S3TC, later adapted to 3D as VTC) provided fast random access for block-based methods [S1, S4]. More recently, methods have adapted standard image compression formats (BCn, ASTC) for volumetric data and explored learned representations like Vector Quantization and Gaussian Splatting [S3, unofficial][S5]. Time-varying data compression has become critical for real-time animation, leveraging temporal coherence [S1, S5].
+Value compression techniques have evolved from simple block truncation methods to sophisticated transform-based and hardware-accelerated schemes. Block Truncation Coding (BTC) and its variants (S3TC, VTC, ETC2, ASTC) are among the most common choices for fixed hardware implementations due to their simple decompression and fast random access, although they exhibit lower signal-to-noise ratios compared to transform methods [S1, S4]. Wavelet transforms, introduced to volume data compression by Muraki [S6, unofficial], became widely adopted because they allow multi-resolution access and efficient energy compaction [S6, unofficial]. Vector Quantization (VQ) and differential coding have been used to exploit spatial and temporal redundancies, respectively [S1, S4]. More recently, methods like Zarr-based archives and neural representation compression (e.g., ReRF) have emerged to handle large-scale and time-varying data with efficient streaming capabilities [S3, unofficial][S9].
 
